menu "Knob power (K7e)"

config KNOB_DIM_AFTER_S
    int "Idle seconds before the backlight dims (0 = never)"
    default 15
    range 0 3600
    help
        First rung of the ladder: the backlight duty drops to
        KNOB_DIM_DUTY after this much touch/dial silence. Any activity
        brings it back to full. P2 made the store authoritative: the
        light-sleep screen's roller (settings.txt idle_timeout_sec)
        drives this rung live, and this value is only the seed the
        supervisor falls back to while the store holds 0.

config KNOB_DIM_DUTY
    int "Dimmed backlight duty (0..255)"
    default 64
    range 1 254
    help
        LEDC duty while dimmed (full = 255, the vendor's "on"). 64 is the
        P1 dim parity value: 25 %, matching the clocks' light-sleep panel
        (UI_LIGHT_SLEEP_DIM_PCT); the shared clock widget dims to
        UI_CLOCK_ALPHA_PCT alongside it (knob_render_set_home_dim).

config KNOB_ALARM_TIMER_WAKE
    bool "Deep sleep: arm the internal RTC timer for the next alarm rule"
    default y
    help
        The user's alarm-across-deep-sleep choice: the knob ALWAYS deep
        sleeps, and the S3's internal RTC timer wakes it at the next
        rule time (+/- the RC drift, bench-checked at <= ~0.5 %). With n
        the knob sleeps GPIO-only (EXT0/EXT1) and alarms fire only while
        awake. The wake decision (fire / hold-to-rule-time / ignore) is
        main.c's arbitration; the never-early-ring rule lives there.

config KNOB_ALARM_GRACE_S
    int "TIMER wake counts as the alarm fire within +/- this many seconds"
    default 300
    range 30 900
    help
        The rule time the RTC timer was armed for, as recomputed from
        the NVS snapshot / SNTP on wake, may differ by the accumulated
        RC drift. Within +/- this window the wake IS the alarm (silent
        alarm visual). Outside it, a TIMER wake is either an early wake
        (hold to rule time, NEVER ring early) or stale (ignore).

config KNOB_ALARM_LATE_MAX_S
    int "Ignore a TIMER alarm wake this far off in either direction"
    default 900
    range 300 7200
    help
        Beyond GRACE the wake is arbitrated: early wakes within LATE_MAX
        wait at 1 Hz for the rule; anything further off (early or late)
        is treated as a stale/garbage timer fire -> a normal dial wake.

config KNOB_ALARM_MAX_SLEEP_GAP_S
    int "Sleep GPIO-only when the next rule is farther out than this"
    default 82800
    range 3600 172800
    help
        A weekday-mask gap (e.g. Friday night -> Monday 7:00 = ~60 h)
        exceeds any sane timer horizon; past this distance the knob
        sleeps on EXT0/EXT1 only and relies on a human/dial wake
        (23 h default covers the weekday case; > MAX means the
        scheduler's next-rule cache is the only timer source).

config KNOB_SLEEP_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Idle seconds before deep sleep (0 = never sleep)"
    default 60
    range 0 36000
    help
        Second rung: esp_deep_sleep_start with EXT0 on dial-A GPIO 8
        (active-LOW), one countdown AFTER the dim rung -- the clock's
        additive posture (sleep at idle_timeout + this countdown, both
        from the last activity). P2 made the store authoritative: the
        deep-sleep screen's roller (settings.txt
        deep_sleep_countdown_sec) drives the countdown live, and this
        value is only the seed the supervisor falls back to while the
        store holds 0. The cold-boot presentation HOLDS the ladder (a
        presentation is never interrupted); the clock's wake policy
        then shows the current phase statically on every boot and wake.

config KNOB_TOUCH_WAKE
    bool "Wake from deep sleep on a screen tap (EXT1 CST816S INT)"
    default y
    help
        Arms EXT1 ANY_LOW on the CST816S INT line (GPIO 9, RTC IO) next
        to the dial-A EXT0, so a tap wakes the knob like a dial detent
        does. Before sleeping, one touch status read clears a pending
        INT pulse (a stale low would re-wake instantly -- the boot-loop
        guard). If the pad rejects EXT1 the supervisor logs it and
        stays dial-only, like KNOB_SLEEP_ALLOW=n does for the sleep
        itself. Bench-verify: tap, then dial, from a dark panel.

config KNOB_SLEEP_ALLOW
    bool "Actually enter deep sleep (n = log-only)"
    default y
    help
        Bench development can keep the device reachable over the
        USB-JTAG serial with n: the supervisor logs the would-be sleep
        (including the EXT0 RTCIO verdict) and stays awake. Production
        ships y. The device page records which mode was benched.

endmenu
